diff --git a/docs/debug-metadata.md b/docs/debug-metadata.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d9d71b7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/debug-metadata.md
@@ -0,0 +1,109 @@
+# Native debug metadata
+
+`--debug-info` now describes original source files in assembler line tables,
+emits DWARF 4 on ELF/Mach-O and CodeView on Windows COFF, and retains the expanded
+`.bpp-source` snapshot for inspection. Debuggers use the original files, so keep
+those files available and unchanged while debugging. The sidecar is no longer
+the source of every location.
+
+## Source provenance
+
+AST nodes, IR instructions, procedure declarations and variable declarations
+carry original file/context IDs and exclusive end positions. Import expansion
+produces a file table, an import-context tree and an expanded-line mapping.
+Lowering copies spans; folding and CFG/DCE compaction retain them with the
+surviving instruction. Synthetic instructions have no original-file ID.
+Parser, lowering and runtime errors use original filenames and coordinates.
+
+`--emit-map` includes `source_files`, `import_contexts`, original instruction
+spans, declared variable types and storage information. DWARF also carries
+import nesting through standard `DW_MACINFO_start_file`/`end_file` records.
+CodeView has original file/line tables; its import-context tree remains in the
+JSON map. Parsed macro declarations/native snippets are currently discarded by
+the language implementation: there is no macro expansion to attribute. A future
+expander must carry both definition and invocation provenance.
+
+## Procedures, scopes and values
+
+Procedure names, parameter names, locals and runtime value types appear in
+standard debug records. B++ blocks do not introduce lexical scopes: variables
+belong to their entire procedure, and module declarations are globals. No nested
+lexical scope is invented. DWARF globals have conservative availability ranges
+in the module entry; they are unavailable in other procedures until an
+interprocedural initialization analysis is implemented. CodeView retains module
+global names/types without definition ranges: its current register-relative
+range form cannot describe dereferencing the runtime global-array pointer.
+
+All source values are dynamically tagged `BLValue` structures (tag plus an
+integer/object-pointer union), including declarations spelled `int`, `String`
+or `Array`. Parameter declarations have no static type. The map preserves
+declared spelling; standard records describe the actual runtime representation.
+DWARF uses C99 to make that structure readable by existing debuggers. Procedures
+are marked `DW_CC_nocall`: their compiler-private physical calling convention
+cannot be invoked using a source-level debugger prototype. CodeView similarly
+uses `T_NOTYPE` for the callable procedure prototype while preserving typed
+source parameters in its local-symbol records. Calling B++ procedures from a
+debugger is unsupported.
+
+Named variables currently have canonical tagged-value slots. Allocated payload
+registers hold caches of IR temporaries, not complete source values. Debug
+records therefore use register-relative canonical addresses (`r12` or `x19`),
+with DWARF location lists and CodeView register-relative definition ranges.
+Do not infer value availability from the allocator's planned cache segments.
+Register-only source variables and register/spill transitions will need actual
+emission events when that allocation strategy is introduced.
+
+Definite slot-owner analysis follows CFG edges and loops. Locations start after
+successful initialization, disappear while a reused slot is initialized for
+another variable, and end before cleanup. Ambiguous joins, uninitialized values,
+and former owners are reported as unavailable/optimized out, rather than reading
+stale frame cells. The analysis is conservative: unavailable does not always
+mean that the value cannot exist on a particular runtime path. Global locations
+use the runtime frame's global-array pointer, not a fictitious static address.
+
+Prologues use their procedure declaration as a non-statement line entry, so
+LLDB recognizes the function before reaching `prologue_end`. Cleanup uses line
+zero; the first source instruction marks `prologue_end`. Existing CFI/SEH remains active through probes, helper
+calls and shared cleanup. ARM64 now restores saved registers before releasing
+their stack storage, preventing asynchronous signal frames from overwriting
+those saved values during the epilogue.
+
+## Validation
+
+From the checkout root, with Clang, LLVM inspection tools, GNU `readelf`, GDB and
+LLDB installed:
+
+```sh
+make CC=clang BUILD_MODE=release check-debug-metadata
+make CC=clang BUILD_MODE=release check-debug-pdb
+```
+
+`check-debug-metadata` tests nested import provenance and spans after optimization,
+inspects all four targets in optimized/baseline modes with `llvm-dwarfdump`,
+`llvm-readobj` and `readelf`, and verifies original-source stepping, live and
+unavailable values, loops, recursive backtraces, entry/cleanup unwinding and
+imported runtime errors with the selected host debuggers. Missing required tools
+fail the explicit gate. Select `BPP_DEBUGGERS=lldb` on macOS, or
+`BPP_DEBUGGERS=none` for object inspection without native debugger execution.
+
+Tool overrides are `BPP_ASSEMBLER`, `BPP_DWARFDUMP`, `BPP_READOBJ`, `BPP_READELF`,
+`BPP_GDB`, `BPP_LLDB`, `BPP_LLD_LINK`, and `BPP_PDBUTIL`.
+`check-debug-pdb` uses LLD and `llvm-pdbutil` to round-trip CodeView into a PDB.
+Its runtime link stubs are never executed: this verifies metadata compatibility,
+not a runnable Windows program.
+
+For checkouts containing tracked build outputs, use separate directories:
+
+```sh
+make CC=cc BUILD_MODE=release OBJDIR=build/onboarding-release/obj \
+  BIN=bin/onboarding-release/bpp check-debug-metadata check-debug-pdb
+```
+
+Linux x86-64 GDB/LLDB execution, all eight cross-object inspections and the PDB
+round trip have been verified during this change. Real macOS ARM64/Linux ARM64
+debugger sessions and Windows WinDbg/Visual Studio sessions remain native-host
+gates. On macOS inspect the linked executable/dSYM with `dwarfdump --verify`;
+on Windows link the real runtime with debug/PDB options and test the PDB in
+WinDbg or Visual Studio. Cross-assembly and a metadata-only PDB do not replace
+those sessions. Signal/probe/veneer instruction-by-instruction unwind sampling
+on every native host also remains a separate verification gate.
